window.VAKSHARA_TEACHER_GUIDES = {"start/03-variables-assignment-and-names":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Variables: storing and reusing information from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Variables: storing and reusing information ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs need to remember information. Variables give values names so the program can reuse and update them.","whyHi":"Programs ko information yaad rakhni hoti hai. Variables values ko names dete hain, taaki program unhe dobara use aur update kar sake.","analogyEn":"A variable is like a labelled box in your notes. The label is the name; what is currently inside is the value. You can replace the contents later, but the label lets you refer to it again.","analogyHi":"Variable notes mein labelled box jaisa hai. Label uska name hai; box ke andar jo hai woh value hai. Baad mein contents replace kar sakte ho, lekin label se value ko phir refer kar sakte ho.","stepsEn":["student_name refers to the string \"Asha\".","score is first assigned the integer 72.","score = score + 5 reads the current value 72, calculates 77, then assigns 77 back to score.","The next print displays 77.","The final assignment makes score refer to the string \"excellent\"."],"stepsHi":["student_name string \"Asha\" ko refer karta hai.","score ko pehle integer 72 assign hota hai.","score = score + 5 current value 72 read karta hai, 77 calculate karta hai aur 77 ko score mein assign karta hai.","Next print 77 display karta hai.","Last assignment ke baad score string \"excellent\" ko refer karta hai."],"code":"student_name = \"Asha\"\nscore = 72\nprint(student_name, score)\n\nscore = score + 5\nprint(score)\n\nscore = \"excellent\"\nprint(score)","output":"Asha 72\n77\nexcellent","mistakeEn":"Do not use = when you mean “compare”. Use == for comparison. Also avoid vague names such as x when a descriptive name like total_marks makes the code easier to understand.","mistakeHi":"Jab compare karna ho toh = nahi, == use karo. x jaise vague names ke bajaye total_marks jaise descriptive names code ko samajhna easy banate hain.","memoryEn":"name = value means “give this name this value”. Python variables are names bound to objects; the labelled-box picture is a beginner-friendly model.","memoryHi":"name = value ka matlab “is name ko ye value do”. Python variables names hote hain jo objects ko refer karte hain; labelled-box model beginner ke liye useful picture hai.","conceptEn":"A variable gives a value a name so your program can use it again. Learn assignment, reassignment, naming rules, case sensitivity and Python’s dynamic typing before moving to detailed data types.","conceptHi":"Variable kisi value ko naam deta hai taaki program use dobara use kar sake. Data types detail mein jaane se pehle assignment, reassignment, naming rules, case sensitivity aur dynamic typing samjho.","deepSectionsEn":[{"title":"A variable is a name for information","body":"Imagine a labelled container in a classroom. The label helps you find the information later. In Python, a variable name refers to a value, so you can use that name again instead of rewriting the value everywhere."},{"title":"The equals sign assigns; it does not ask a question","body":"In score = 72, Python evaluates 72 and binds the name score to it. A comparison asks whether two values are equal; that uses ==. These symbols look similar but perform different jobs."},{"title":"Reassignment updates the value associated with a name","body":"When you write score = 85, the name score now refers to 85. Python does not keep the old value under the same name for normal use. You can also assign a value of another type later; Python is dynamically typed."},{"title":"Good names help you read your own code","body":"A name such as total_marks tells the reader what the value represents. Names cannot start with a digit, cannot contain spaces or hyphens, and are case-sensitive. Use lowercase words separated by underscores for ordinary variable names."}],"deepSectionsHi":[{"title":"Variable information ka name hota hai","body":"Classroom mein labelled box socho. Label se baad mein information find karna easy hota hai. Python mein variable name kisi value ko refer karta hai, isliye same value ko baar-baar type karne ki zaroorat nahi hoti."},{"title":"Equals sign assign karta hai; question nahi poochta","body":"score = 72 mein Python 72 evaluate karke score name ko us value se bind karta hai. Comparison ka matlab hota hai poochhna ki do values equal hain ya nahi; uske liye == use hota hai. Dono symbols similar dikhte hain, par kaam alag hai."},{"title":"Reassignment se name ki current value update hoti hai","body":"Jab tum score = 85 likhte ho, score ab 85 ko refer karta hai. Normal use mein same name ke neeche old value automatically preserve nahi hoti. Baad mein different type ki value bhi assign kar sakte ho; Python dynamically typed hai."},{"title":"Achhe names se code padhna easy hota hai","body":"total_marks jaise name se reader ko pata chalta hai value kya represent karti hai. Name digit se start nahi ho sakta, spaces/hyphens nahi ho sakte aur names case-sensitive hote hain. Normal variables ke liye lowercase words ke beech underscores use karo."}]},"start/02-first-program-print-comments-indentation":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Your first Python program: print, comments and indentation from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Your first Python program: print, comments and indentation ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"A first program teaches the small rules used everywhere: statements, text, output, comments, punctuation and indentation.","whyHi":"First program se woh chhote rules seekhte hain jo har jagah use honge: statements, text, output, comments, punctuation aur indentation.","analogyEn":"Imagine giving someone directions. The order matters: first open the door, then walk inside. In Python, statements run from top to bottom unless a condition, loop or function changes the flow. Indentation shows which instructions belong together.","analogyHi":"Kisi ko directions dena socho. Order matter karta hai: pehle door kholo, phir andar jao. Python mein statements top-to-bottom run hote hain, jab tak condition/loop/function flow change na kare. Indentation batati hai kaunsi instructions ek block ka part hain.","stepsEn":["Python ignores the comment line because it begins with #.","The assignment gives the name name the text value \"Asha\".","print() displays Hello and Asha, separated by a space.","Python checks whether marks >= 40. The result is True.","The two indented lines run as part of the if block.","The final print is outside the block, so it runs afterward."],"stepsHi":["Python # se start hone wali comment line ko execute nahi karta.","Assignment name variable ko \"Asha\" text value deta hai.","print() Hello aur Asha ko space ke saath display karta hai.","Python check karta hai marks >= 40. Result True hai.","Dono indented lines if block ke part ke roop mein run hoti hain.","Last print block ke bahar hai, isliye baad mein run hota hai."],"code":"# A comment explains the code\nname = \"Asha\"\nprint(\"Hello\", name)\n\nmarks = 82\nif marks >= 40:\n    print(\"Pass\")\n    print(\"Keep learning\")\nprint(\"Result checked\")","output":"Hello Asha\nPass\nKeep learning\nResult checked","mistakeEn":"Do not remove the indentation under if, for or while. Python uses indentation to know which statements are inside the block.","mistakeHi":"if, for ya while ke andar wali indentation remove mat karo. Python indentation se samajhta hai kaunsi statements block ke andar hain.","memoryEn":"Quotes mean text. # means comment. Indentation means “this line belongs to the block above”.","memoryHi":"Quotes ka matlab text. # ka matlab comment. Indentation ka matlab “ye line upar wale block ke andar hai”.","conceptEn":"Now that you know where Python runs, write a tiny program and learn how to read it. We will cover print(), strings, comments, statement order and indentation.","conceptHi":"Ab jab pata hai Python kahan run hota hai, ek chhota program likhenge aur usko read karna seekhenge. print(), strings, comments, statement order aur indentation cover karenge.","deepSectionsEn":[{"title":"print() displays; it does not change the value","body":"When you call print(\"Hello\"), Python writes Hello to the output area. When you call print(10 + 5), Python calculates 15 first and displays that result. The function is showing the value, not storing it in a variable."},{"title":"Quotes tell Python that something is text","body":"The quotation marks are not part of the displayed text. They tell Python where the string starts and ends. A number without quotes can be used in arithmetic; the same digits inside quotes are text."},{"title":"Indentation is part of Python syntax","body":"In many languages braces mark a block. Python uses indentation. Lines with the same indentation inside a block belong together. A missing or inconsistent indentation can cause an IndentationError or change which statements run."}],"deepSectionsHi":[{"title":"print() display karta hai; value change nahi karta","body":"Jab print(\"Hello\") likhte ho, Python output area mein Hello dikhata hai. print(10 + 5) mein Python pehle 15 calculate karta hai aur phir show karta hai. print value ko display karta hai, variable mein store nahi karta."},{"title":"Quotes Python ko batate hain ki ye text hai","body":"Quotation marks output ka part nahi hote. Ye Python ko batate hain ki string kahan start aur end hoti hai. Quotes ke bina number arithmetic mein use ho sakta hai; same digits quotes ke andar text hain."},{"title":"Indentation Python syntax ka part hai","body":"Kuch languages blocks ke liye braces use karti hain. Python indentation use karta hai. Block ke andar same indentation wali lines ek group mein hoti hain. Indentation missing ya inconsistent hui toh IndentationError aa sakta hai ya statements ka flow change ho sakta hai."}]},"start/01-run-python-and-use-colab":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Where and how can you run Python? from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Where and how can you run Python? ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Writing code is only one part of learning. You also need a safe place to run it, see the output, change it and run it again.","whyHi":"Code likhna learning ka ek part hai. Use run karne, output dekhne, change karne aur dobara run karne ke liye workspace bhi chahiye.","analogyEn":"A notebook is like a workbook with separate boxes. You write one small experiment in a cell, run that cell and see the result directly underneath. You do not need to build a whole project before trying your first line.","analogyHi":"Notebook workbook jaisi hai jisme alag boxes hote hain. Ek cell mein chhota experiment likho, cell run karo aur result uske neeche dekho. First line try karne ke liye poora project banana zaroori nahi.","stepsEn":["Open a notebook and select a code cell.","Type the Python statements.","Run the cell using its Run control or Shift + Enter.","Read the output shown underneath.","If another cell uses a variable, run the cell that creates that variable first."],"stepsHi":["Notebook open karke code cell select karo.","Python statements type karo.","Run button ya Shift + Enter se cell run karo.","Neeche dikhne wala output padho.","Agar next cell variable use karta hai, toh variable banane wala cell pehle run karo."],"code":"print(\"First cell\")\nprint(2 + 3)","output":"First cell\n5","mistakeEn":"If you run cells out of order, an earlier variable may not exist yet—or it may contain an older value. For a clean check, use Runtime → Run all in a fresh notebook session.","mistakeHi":"Cells ko out of order run karoge toh pehle define na hua variable missing ho sakta hai ya purani value ho sakti hai. Clean check ke liye fresh session mein Runtime → Run all use karo.","memoryEn":"Write in a cell → run the cell → read the output. A notebook lets you learn in small experiments.","memoryHi":"Cell mein likho → cell run karo → output padho. Notebook chhote experiments ke through seekhne deta hai.","conceptEn":"A Python program needs a place to be written and an environment that can run it. Your supplied learning material uses Google Colab notebooks, so we begin with cells and outputs, then explain the local-editor idea.","conceptHi":"Python program likhne ke liye jagah aur run karne ke liye environment chahiye. Tumhare learning material mein Google Colab notebooks use hue hain, isliye pehle cells aur outputs samjhenge, phir local editor ka idea.","deepSectionsEn":[{"title":"A browser notebook is a learning workspace","body":"It lets you put a small code experiment in one cell and see its output directly underneath. You can keep explanations next to code. This makes it easier to test one idea at a time."},{"title":"A running session remembers names","body":"After you run score = 80, the current Python session knows the name score. A later cell can use it. Restarting the runtime clears those stored names, so run the cells that create values again."},{"title":"A notebook and a .py file are both useful","body":"Use a notebook when you want to experiment and explain results step by step. Use a Python file when you want to organise a reusable program. The Python language is the same; the working environment is different."}],"deepSectionsHi":[{"title":"Browser notebook learning workspace hoti hai","body":"Isme ek cell mein chhota code experiment likhkar uska output bilkul neeche dekh sakte ho. Explanation aur code paas-paas rehte hain, isliye ek idea ko test karna easy hota hai."},{"title":"Running session names ko yaad rakhta hai","body":"Jab tum score = 80 run karte ho, current Python session score name ko jaanta hai. Next cell use kar sakta hai. Runtime restart karne par stored names clear ho jaate hain, isliye values banane wale cells dobara run karo."},{"title":"Notebook aur .py file dono useful hain","body":"Notebook tab use karo jab experiments aur outputs ko step-by-step explain karna ho. Reusable program organise karne ke liye Python file use karo. Language same hai; working environment alag hai."}]},"start/00-what-is-programming-and-python":{"titleEn":"Let's understand What is programming, and what is Python? from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo What is programming, and what is Python? ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Every app, calculator and website follows instructions. Programming is how we write those instructions clearly so a computer can perform a task.","whyHi":"Apps, calculator aur websites sab instructions follow karte hain. Programming se hum computer ke liye woh instructions clearly likhte hain.","analogyEn":"Think of a recipe. A recipe tells a cook what to do, in what order and with which ingredients. A program does the same for a computer: it gives precise instructions, in a language the computer can run.","analogyHi":"Recipe socho. Recipe cook ko batati hai kya karna hai, kis order mein aur kaunse ingredients ke saath. Program bhi computer ko precise instructions deta hai, bas programming language mein.","stepsEn":["Python starts at the first line.","print() displays the text inside quotation marks.","Python moves to the next line and displays the second message.","For 2 + 3, Python calculates the expression first.","print() displays the calculated result: 5."],"stepsHi":["Python first line se start karta hai.","print() quotation marks ke andar ka text display karta hai.","Python next line par jaakar doosra message display karta hai.","2 + 3 ke case mein Python pehle calculation karta hai.","print() calculated result 5 display karta hai."],"code":"print(\"Hello, VAKSHARA!\")\nprint(\"I am learning Python.\")\nprint(2 + 3)","output":"Hello, VAKSHARA!\nI am learning Python.\n5","mistakeEn":"Do not think a computer understands everyday English instructions automatically. Code must follow Python syntax and exact rules.","mistakeHi":"Computer normal English instruction automatically nahi samajhta. Code ko Python syntax aur exact rules follow karne hote hain.","memoryEn":"Programming = problem + ordered instructions + checking the result. Python is the language we are using to write those instructions.","memoryHi":"Programming = problem + order mein instructions + result check karna. Python woh language hai jisme hum instructions likh rahe hain.","conceptEn":"Before learning symbols and syntax, understand the big picture: programming means writing clear instructions that a computer can follow. Python is one language used to write those instructions.","conceptHi":"Syntax se pehle big picture samjho: programming ka matlab computer ke liye clear instructions likhna hai. Python un instructions ko likhne ki ek language hai.","deepSectionsEn":[{"title":"A program is a set of exact instructions","body":"A computer does not fill in missing steps like a person might. When we write a program, we decide the order and describe each action clearly. Even a tiny two-line program is a real program."},{"title":"Python is the language, not the task","body":"“Python” names the language we write. The task is the problem we want to solve. For example, “calculate a student average” is the task; Python is one tool we can use to write the instructions."},{"title":"The first program, one line at a time","body":"The first print() displays a text message. The second print() displays another message. The third print() receives the expression 2 + 3, calculates it, and displays the number 5. Text and numbers are different kinds of values; we will study them carefully in later lessons."},{"title":"What to do when an error appears","body":"Read the error message slowly. Check the line it points to, then inspect spelling, brackets, quotation marks and indentation. Change one thing at a time and run the program again."}],"deepSectionsHi":[{"title":"Program exact instructions ka set hota hai","body":"Computer insaan ki tarah missing steps khud se complete nahi karta. Program mein hum action ka order clearly likhte hain. Sirf do lines ka code bhi ek real program hota hai."},{"title":"Python language hai, task nahi","body":"Python us language ka naam hai jisme hum instructions likhenge. Task alag cheez hai. Jaise “student ka average nikalna” task hai; Python us task ko solve karne ka tool ho sakta hai."},{"title":"First program ko line-by-line samjho","body":"Pehla print() ek text message dikhata hai. Doosra print() next message dikhata hai. Teesre print() ke andar 2 + 3 expression hai; Python pehle calculate karta hai aur phir number 5 display karta hai. Text aur number alag values hain; unhe next lessons mein detail se samjhenge."},{"title":"Error aaye toh kya karein?","body":"Error message ko slowly padho. Jis line ka naam diya hai use check karo. Spelling, brackets, quotation marks aur indentation dekho. Ek baar mein ek change karo aur phir run karo."}]},"foundations/01-data-types":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Data types and Python values from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Data types and Python values ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"Think of a school bag: it can hold a book, a water bottle, a name card and a yes/no permission slip. They are all values, but they are different kinds of values.","analogyHi":"School bag mein book, water bottle, name card aur yes/no permission slip ho sakti hai. Sab values hain, lekin unka type alag hai.","stepsEn":["18 is a whole number, so Python creates an int value.","1.65 has a decimal part, so its type is float.","Text inside quotes is a str; True is a bool value."],"stepsHi":["18 whole number hai, isliye iska type int hai.","1.65 mein decimal part hai, isliye float hai.","Quotes ke andar text str hota hai; True ek bool value hai."],"code":"age = 18\nheight = 1.65\nname = \"Asha\"\nis_student = True\n\nprint(type(age).__name__)\nprint(type(height).__name__)\nprint(type(name).__name__)\nprint(type(is_student).__name__)","output":"int\nfloat\nstr\nbool","mistakeEn":"Do not confuse 25 with \"25\". The first is a number you can calculate with; the second is text.","mistakeHi":"25 aur \"25\" ko same mat samjho. Pehla number hai jiske saath calculation ho sakti hai; doosra text hai.","memoryEn":"Ask two questions: what value is stored, and what type is that value?","memoryHi":"Do sawal poochho: value kya hai, aur us value ka type kya hai?","conceptEn":"A variable stores a reference to a value. The value has a type, and that type determines which operations make sense. Start by recognising integers, floats, strings, booleans and complex numbers.","conceptHi":"Variable ek value ko refer karta hai. Value ka type batata hai ki us par kaunse operations meaningful hain. Pehle int, float, str, bool aur complex ko clearly pehchanna seekhenge."},"foundations/02-input-and-type-casting":{"titleEn":"Let's understand User input and type conversion from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo User input and type conversion ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"A form collects what a person writes. Even if someone writes 18 in an age box, the form first receives characters. Your program must convert that text into a number before doing maths.","analogyHi":"Form mein user jo likhta hai, woh pehle text hota hai. Age box mein 18 likhne par bhi input() ko \"18\" string milti hai. Calculation se pehle number mein convert karna padta hai.","stepsEn":["age_text is the string \"18\".","int() converts that text into the integer 18.","float() converts decimal text into 1.65, so arithmetic is now possible."],"stepsHi":["age_text mein \"18\" text ke roop mein hai.","int() us text ko integer 18 mein convert karta hai.","float() decimal text ko 1.65 number banata hai; ab calculation ho sakti hai."],"code":"age_text = \"18\"\nage = int(age_text)\nheight_text = \"1.65\"\nheight = float(height_text)\n\nprint(age + 2)\nprint(height + 0.10)","output":"20\n1.75","mistakeEn":"input() does not automatically return a number. Convert it, and handle invalid input because int(\"eighteen\") raises ValueError.","mistakeHi":"input() apne aap number nahi deta. Convert karo; galat text par int(\"eighteen\") ValueError dega.","memoryEn":"input → text → convert → calculate. Remember this order.","memoryHi":"input → text → conversion → calculation. Ye order yaad rakho.","conceptEn":"input() reads what a user types and returns it as text. If a program needs a number, convert that text with int() or float() before doing arithmetic.","conceptHi":"input() user ka typed data text ke roop mein deta hai. Agar calculation karni hai, toh int() ya float() se convert karke hi arithmetic karein."},"foundations/03-concatenation-and-first-calculation":{"titleEn":"Let's understand String concatenation and a first calculation from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo String concatenation and a first calculation ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"The plus sign has a job based on the values around it. With two pieces of text it joins them like two name tags; with two numbers it adds their amounts.","analogyHi":"Plus sign ka kaam uske dono taraf ki values par depend karta hai. Do strings ko jodta hai; do numbers ko add karta hai.","stepsEn":["first and second are strings, so + concatenates them.","length and width are numbers, so * performs multiplication.","The rectangle area is 12 × 4 = 48 square units."],"stepsHi":["first aur second strings hain, isliye + unhe join karta hai.","length aur width numbers hain, isliye * multiplication karta hai.","Rectangle ka area 12 × 4 = 48 square units hai."],"code":"first = \"VAK\"\nsecond = \"SHARA\"\nprint(first + second)\n\nlength = 12\nwidth = 4\narea = length * width\nprint(area)","output":"VAKSHARA\n48","mistakeEn":"\"12\" + \"4\" gives \"124\", not 16. Convert to numbers before arithmetic.","mistakeHi":"\"12\" + \"4\" ka result \"124\" hai, 16 nahi. Arithmetic se pehle number mein convert karo.","memoryEn":"Same symbol, different types: string + string joins; number + number adds.","memoryHi":"Same symbol ka result type par depend karta hai: string + string join, number + number add.","conceptEn":"The plus sign can join strings, while arithmetic operators calculate numeric values. Keep text and numbers distinct, and convert deliberately when combining them.","conceptHi":"Plus sign strings ko join kar sakta hai aur numbers ko add. Text aur number alag types hain; combine karne se pehle zarurat ke hisaab se conversion karein."},"foundations/04-python-operators":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Python operators from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Python operators ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"An operator is like an action sign on a calculator. The values are the inputs, the operator tells Python what action to perform, and the expression produces a result.","analogyHi":"Operator calculator ke action button jaisa hai. Values inputs hain, operator action batata hai aur expression result deta hai.","stepsEn":["250 × 2 calculates the total cost: 500.",">= compares the total with 500 and returns True.","and is True only because both comparisons are True."],"stepsHi":["250 × 2 se total 500 aata hai.",">= total ko 500 se compare karta hai aur True deta hai.","and tab True hai kyunki dono conditions True hain."],"code":"price = 250\nquantity = 2\ntotal = price * quantity\nprint(total)\nprint(total >= 500)\nprint(total >= 500 and quantity > 1)","output":"500\nTrue\nTrue","mistakeEn":"Use == to compare values and = to assign a value. They are not interchangeable.","mistakeHi":"= value assign karta hai; == comparison karta hai. Dono ko interchange nahi kar sakte.","memoryEn":"Arithmetic calculates, comparison answers True/False, logical operators combine decisions.","memoryHi":"Arithmetic calculation karta hai, comparison True/False deta hai, logical operator decisions combine karta hai.","conceptEn":"Operators let values participate in calculations, comparisons and logical decisions. Learn what each family returns, not just how its symbol looks.","conceptHi":"Operators values par calculation, comparison aur logical decision karne dete hain. Sirf symbol nahi, har operator ka result samajhna important hai."},"foundations/05-binary-and-bitwise":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Binary numbers and bitwise operators from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Binary numbers and bitwise operators ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"Imagine two rows of tiny switches. Each switch is either OFF (0) or ON (1). A bitwise operator compares switches in matching positions.","analogyHi":"Do rows mein chhote switches socho. Har switch OFF (0) ya ON (1) hota hai. Bitwise operator same position wale switches compare karta hai.","stepsEn":["5 is 0101 and 3 is 0011 when written using four bits.","AND keeps a position only when both bits are 1: 0001 = 1.","OR keeps a position when either bit is 1: 0111 = 7. XOR keeps different bits: 0110 = 6."],"stepsHi":["5 ko 4 bits mein 0101 aur 3 ko 0011 likhte hain.","AND mein dono bits 1 hon tabhi 1 milta hai: 0001 = 1.","OR mein kisi ek bit ke 1 hone par 1 milta hai: 0111 = 7. XOR different bits par 1 deta hai: 0110 = 6."],"code":"a = 5       # binary: 0101\nb = 3       # binary: 0011\nprint(a & b)\nprint(a | b)\nprint(a ^ b)","output":"1\n7\n6","mistakeEn":"Do not confuse and/or (Boolean logic) with &/| (bitwise operations). They are different operators.","mistakeHi":"and/or Boolean logic hain; &/| bitwise operators hain. Dono alag hain.","memoryEn":"AND = both; OR = at least one; XOR = different. Align bits before calculating.","memoryHi":"AND = dono; OR = kam se kam ek; XOR = different. Calculation se pehle bits align karo.","conceptEn":"Bitwise operators work on the binary representation of integers. Visualise the bits in aligned positions before applying AND, OR, XOR, NOT or shifts.","conceptHi":"Bitwise operators integers ke binary bits par kaam karte hain. AND, OR, XOR, NOT ya shift lagane se pehle bits ko position ke hisaab se align karke dekhein."},"foundations/06-identity-complex-and-print":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Identity, swapping, complex numbers and output from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Identity, swapping, complex numbers and output ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Before a program can make decisions or repeat work, it needs values, names, input, output and operators. These are the building blocks used in almost every Python program.","whyHi":"Program decisions le ya kaam repeat kare, usse pehle values, names, input, output aur operators samajhne zaroori hain. Ye almost har Python program ke building blocks hain.","analogyEn":"Equality is asking whether two notebooks contain the same answers. Identity is asking whether both names point to the very same physical notebook.","analogyHi":"Equality poochti hai ki do notebooks mein content same hai ya nahi. Identity poochti hai ki dono names ek hi physical notebook ko point kar rahe hain ya nahi.","stepsEn":["a and b contain equal values, so == is True.","They are separate list objects, so a is b is False.","c refers to the same object as a, so a is c is True.","sep changes the text printed between values."],"stepsHi":["a aur b ki values same hain, isliye == True hai.","Dono alag list objects hain, isliye a is b False hai.","c, a wale same object ko refer karta hai, isliye a is c True hai.","sep print mein values ke beech ka separator badalta hai."],"code":"a = [1, 2]\nb = [1, 2]\nc = a\nprint(a == b)\nprint(a is b)\nprint(a is c)\n\nname, age = \"Asha\", 18\nprint(name, age, sep=\" - \")","output":"True\nFalse\nTrue\nAsha - 18","mistakeEn":"Do not use is to compare ordinary numbers or strings by value. Use == for equality; use is mainly for identity checks such as value is None.","mistakeHi":"Numbers ya strings ki value compare karne ke liye is use mat karo. == use karo; is ka common use value is None hai.","memoryEn":"== asks “same value?”; is asks “same object?”.","memoryHi":"== poochta hai “value same?”; is poochta hai “object same?”","conceptEn":"This group connects object identity, multiple assignment, complex-number values and formatted output. One key distinction: equality compares values; identity asks whether two references point to the same object.","conceptHi":"Is topic group mein object identity, variable swapping, complex numbers aur print formatting samjhenge. Equality values compare karti hai; identity check karti hai ki dono references same object ko point karte hain ya nahi."},"conditions/01-conditions-and-if-family":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Conditions and if statements from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Conditions and if statements ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Real programs react to information. A result may depend on a mark, age, password, stock count or whether a list is empty. Conditions let Python choose the correct path.","whyHi":"Real programs information ke hisaab se react karte hain. Result marks, age, password, stock ya list empty hone par depend kar sakta hai. Conditions Python ko sahi path choose karne deti hain.","analogyEn":"A condition is like a question at a school gate: “Do you have your ID card?” If the answer is yes, one action happens; otherwise another path may happen.","analogyHi":"Condition school gate ke sawal jaisi hai: “ID card hai?” Answer yes ho toh ek action, warna doosra path.","stepsEn":["Python checks marks >= 90. It is False.","Python checks marks >= 60. It is True, so grade becomes B.","The else block is skipped because one earlier branch matched."],"stepsHi":["Python pehle marks >= 90 check karta hai. Ye False hai.","Phir marks >= 60 check hota hai. Ye True hai, isliye grade B set hota hai.","Ek branch match ho gayi, isliye else run nahi hota."],"code":"marks = 72\nif marks >= 90:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif marks >= 60:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"C\"\nprint(grade)","output":"B","mistakeEn":"Put the most restrictive condition first. If you check marks >= 40 before marks >= 90, the A branch may never be reached.","mistakeHi":"Sabse strict condition pehle rakho. Agar marks >= 40 pehle check karoge toh A wali condition kabhi nahi chalegi.","memoryEn":"if checks the first path, elif checks another path, else catches the remaining case.","memoryHi":"if pehla path check karta hai, elif agla, else bachi hui situation handle karta hai.","conceptEn":"A condition is an expression Python can evaluate as True or False. if, else and elif let a program choose which block should run.","conceptHi":"Condition aisi expression hoti hai jiska result True ya False hota hai. if, else aur elif ki madad se program decide karta hai ki kaunsa block run hoga."},"conditions/02-logical-and-nested-conditions":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Logical operators and nested conditions from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Logical operators and nested conditions ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Real programs react to information. A result may depend on a mark, age, password, stock count or whether a list is empty. Conditions let Python choose the correct path.","whyHi":"Real programs information ke hisaab se react karte hain. Result marks, age, password, stock ya list empty hone par depend kar sakta hai. Conditions Python ko sahi path choose karne deti hain.","analogyEn":"A nested decision is like entering a building: first the outside gate must open; only then do you reach the inner room and answer its question.","analogyHi":"Nested decision building ke gates jaisa hai: pehle outer gate pass hoga, tabhi andar ka question check hoga.","stepsEn":["The outer if checks has_id. It is True, so Python enters it.","Inside, the inner if checks has_ticket. It is also True.","The program prints Entry allowed."],"stepsHi":["Outer if mein has_id True hai, isliye Python andar jaata hai.","Andar inner if has_ticket ko check karta hai. Ye bhi True hai.","Isliye Entry allowed print hota hai."],"code":"has_id = True\nhas_ticket = True\nif has_id:\n    if has_ticket:\n        print(\"Entry allowed\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Ticket needed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"ID needed\")","output":"Entry allowed","mistakeEn":"Indentation decides which if belongs inside another if. A wrongly indented else can attach to a different if.","mistakeHi":"Indentation decide karti hai ki kaunsa if kis ke andar hai. Galat indentation se else galat if ke saath attach ho sakta hai.","memoryEn":"and = both checks must pass; or = at least one; nested if = one decision inside another.","memoryHi":"and = dono pass; or = kam se kam ek pass; nested if = ek decision ke andar doosra.","conceptEn":"Logical operators combine conditions. A nested if places one decision inside another, so the second check happens only when the outer branch reaches it.","conceptHi":"Logical operators multiple conditions ko combine karte hain. Nested if mein ek decision ke andar doosra decision hota hai; inner check tabhi hota hai jab outer condition us tak pahunchti hai."},"conditions/03-truthy-falsy-and-mistakes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Truthy, falsy values and common mistakes from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Truthy, falsy values and common mistakes ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Real programs react to information. A result may depend on a mark, age, password, stock count or whether a list is empty. Conditions let Python choose the correct path.","whyHi":"Real programs information ke hisaab se react karte hain. Result marks, age, password, stock ya list empty hone par depend kar sakta hai. Conditions Python ko sahi path choose karne deti hain.","analogyEn":"A program often needs to decide whether something is present. An empty basket means “nothing here”; a basket with one apple means “something is here”. Python has the same idea for truth values.","analogyHi":"Program ko kabhi check karna hota hai ki kuchh present hai ya nahi. Khali tokri mein kuchh nahi; ek apple wali tokri mein kuchh hai. Python mein empty/non-empty values ka truth value bhi hota hai.","stepsEn":["An empty list is falsy.","An empty string is falsy.","A non-empty list is truthy."],"stepsHi":["Empty list falsy hoti hai.","Empty string falsy hoti hai.","Non-empty list truthy hoti hai."],"code":"values = []\nname = \"\"\nsubjects = [\"Python\"]\nprint(bool(values))\nprint(bool(name))\nprint(bool(subjects))","output":"False\nFalse\nTrue","mistakeEn":"Do not write if value == True when you only need a truth test. Usually write if value: or if not value:.","mistakeHi":"Sirf truth check karna ho toh if value == True zaroori nahi. Aksar if value: ya if not value: likho.","memoryEn":"Empty built-in containers and empty text are falsy; non-empty ones are truthy.","memoryHi":"Empty built-in containers aur empty text falsy hote hain; non-empty values truthy.","conceptEn":"Python conditions accept more than literal True and False. Some values are falsy, such as 0, empty strings and empty collections; most other values are truthy. Branch order and indentation also matter.","conceptHi":"Python conditions mein sirf True/False hi nahi, doosri values bhi use ho sakti hain. 0, empty string aur empty collections falsy hoti hain; aksar baaki values truthy hoti hain. Branch order aur indentation bhi important hain."},"loops/01-loop-basics-for-range-while":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Loop basics: for, range and while from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Loop basics: for, range and while ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When an action must happen for many items or many times, copying the same lines creates clutter and mistakes. Loops express the repetition once and let Python repeat it.","whyHi":"Jab ek kaam bahut items ya baar-baar karna ho, same lines copy karna messy aur mistake-prone ho jata hai. Loop repetition ko ek baar likhne deta hai.","analogyEn":"A loop is like checking every student name on an attendance sheet. You repeat the same action for each item instead of writing the action again and again.","analogyHi":"Loop attendance sheet par har student ka naam check karne jaisa hai. Har item ke liye same action repeat hota hai.","stepsEn":["range(1, 4) produces 1, 2 and 3; the stop value 4 is not included.","The for loop takes one value at a time and prints it.","The while loop repeats while count <= 3 remains True; count += 1 moves it toward stopping."],"stepsHi":["range(1, 4) se 1, 2, 3 milte hain; 4 include nahi hota.","for loop ek-ek value lekar print karta hai.","while tab tak repeat hota hai jab tak count <= 3 True hai; count += 1 loop ko end ki taraf le jaata hai."],"code":"for number in range(1, 4):\n    print(number)\n\ncount = 1\nwhile count <= 3:\n    print(count)\n    count += 1","output":"1\n2\n3\n1\n2\n3","mistakeEn":"A while loop must change something that can eventually make its condition False. Otherwise it may run forever.","mistakeHi":"while loop mein aisi value update honi chahiye jo condition ko ek din False bana de. Warna infinite loop ho sakta hai.","memoryEn":"for is handy when you have a sequence; while is handy when repetition depends on a condition.","memoryHi":"Sequence ho toh for useful; repeat karna condition par depend ho toh while useful.","conceptEn":"A loop repeats instructions. Use for when iterating over a sequence or range, and while when repetition depends on a condition changing over time.","conceptHi":"Loop instructions ko repeat karta hai. Sequence ya range ke items par chalna ho toh for; condition ke badalne tak repeat karna ho toh while use hota hai."},"loops/02-tracing-counters-accumulators":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Trace loops, counters and accumulators from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Trace loops, counters and accumulators ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When an action must happen for many items or many times, copying the same lines creates clutter and mistakes. Loops express the repetition once and let Python repeat it.","whyHi":"Jab ek kaam bahut items ya baar-baar karna ho, same lines copy karna messy aur mistake-prone ho jata hai. Loop repetition ko ek baar likhne deta hai.","analogyEn":"A counter is a tally mark that counts how many things happened. An accumulator is a running total in a notebook where you keep adding each new amount.","analogyHi":"Counter tally mark jaisa hai jo count rakhta hai. Accumulator running total hai jisme har nayi value add hoti rehti hai.","stepsEn":["Start total at 0.","Add 2: total becomes 2.","Add 4: total becomes 6; add 6: total becomes 12."],"stepsHi":["total ko 0 se start karo.","2 add hua: total 2 ho gaya.","4 add hua: total 6; phir 6 add hua: total 12."],"code":"total = 0\nfor number in [2, 4, 6]:\n    total += number\n    print(\"running total:\", total)\nprint(\"final:\", total)","output":"running total: 2\nrunning total: 6\nrunning total: 12\nfinal: 12","mistakeEn":"If total is meant to grow, do not reset it to 0 inside the loop. That would erase earlier work on every iteration.","mistakeHi":"Agar total grow karna hai toh loop ke andar har baar 0 mat karo. Isse purana total erase ho jayega.","memoryEn":"Counter usually changes by 1; accumulator collects a running result.","memoryHi":"Counter aam taur par 1 se badhta hai; accumulator running result collect karta hai.","conceptEn":"A dry run follows variable values one iteration at a time. A counter tracks how many events occur; an accumulator keeps a running result such as a sum.","conceptHi":"Dry run mein har iteration ke baad variables ki value track karte hain. Counter events ki ginti rakhta hai; accumulator running result jaise total sum store karta hai."},"loops/03-break-continue-loop-else":{"titleEn":"Let's understand break, continue and loop else from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo break, continue and loop else ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When an action must happen for many items or many times, copying the same lines creates clutter and mistakes. Loops express the repetition once and let Python repeat it.","whyHi":"Jab ek kaam bahut items ya baar-baar karna ho, same lines copy karna messy aur mistake-prone ho jata hai. Loop repetition ko ek baar likhne deta hai.","analogyEn":"In a classroom line, “skip this student for this activity” is like continue. “Stop the whole activity now” is like break.","analogyHi":"Class ki line mein kisi ek student ko current activity se skip karna continue jaisa hai. Puri activity rok dena break jaisa hai.","stepsEn":["At 1, neither condition matches, so print 1.","At 2, continue skips the remaining body and starts the next iteration.","At 4, break exits the loop completely; 4 is not printed."],"stepsHi":["1 par dono conditions false hain, isliye 1 print hota hai.","2 par continue current iteration ka baaki code skip karta hai.","4 par break poora loop stop kar deta hai; 4 print nahi hota."],"code":"for number in range(1, 6):\n    if number == 2:\n        continue\n    if number == 4:\n        break\n    print(number)","output":"1\n3","mistakeEn":"break exits the nearest loop; continue skips only the current iteration. They do not do the same job.","mistakeHi":"break nearest loop ko exit karta hai; continue sirf current iteration skip karta hai. Dono ka kaam alag hai.","memoryEn":"continue = skip this round; break = stop the loop.","memoryHi":"continue = ye round skip; break = loop band.","conceptEn":"These statements change the normal loop flow. break exits the nearest loop, continue skips to the next iteration, and loop else runs only when the loop finishes without break.","conceptHi":"Ye statements loop ka normal flow change karte hain. break nearest loop se bahar nikalta hai, continue next iteration par le jata hai, aur loop else tab chalta hai jab loop break ke bina finish ho."},"loops/04-nested-loops-and-number-digits":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Nested loops and processing number digits from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Nested loops and processing number digits ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When an action must happen for many items or many times, copying the same lines creates clutter and mistakes. Loops express the repetition once and let Python repeat it.","whyHi":"Jab ek kaam bahut items ya baar-baar karna ho, same lines copy karna messy aur mistake-prone ho jata hai. Loop repetition ko ek baar likhne deta hai.","analogyEn":"A nested loop is like rows and seats in a theatre. The outer loop chooses a row; the inner loop visits every seat in that row.","analogyHi":"Nested loop theatre ki rows aur seats jaisa hai. Outer loop row choose karta hai; inner loop us row ki har seat visit karta hai.","stepsEn":["The outer loop starts at row 1.","The inner loop completes seats 1, 2 and 3 before the outer loop moves on.","Then row 2 repeats the same inner sequence."],"stepsHi":["Outer loop row 1 se start hota hai.","Inner loop seats 1, 2, 3 complete karta hai; tab outer loop next row par jaata hai.","Row 2 ke liye inner loop phir se poora chalta hai."],"code":"for row in range(1, 3):\n    for seat in range(1, 4):\n        print(row, seat)","output":"1 1\n1 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 2\n2 3","mistakeEn":"A nested loop can run many times: 2 outer rounds × 3 inner rounds = 6 prints here.","mistakeHi":"Nested loops mein total repetitions multiply hote hain: yahan 2 outer × 3 inner = 6 prints.","memoryEn":"The inner loop finishes all its rounds for each single outer-loop round.","memoryHi":"Outer loop ke ek round ke andar inner loop apne saare rounds complete karta hai.","conceptEn":"A nested loop places one loop inside another. Processing digits commonly uses remainder (%) to read the last digit and floor division (//) to remove it.","conceptHi":"Nested loop mein ek loop doosre loop ke andar hota hai. Number ke digits process karne ke liye % se last digit nikalte hain aur // se last digit hataate hain."},"loops/05-loop-mistakes-and-input-controlled-loops":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Loop mistakes and input-controlled loops from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Loop mistakes and input-controlled loops ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When an action must happen for many items or many times, copying the same lines creates clutter and mistakes. Loops express the repetition once and let Python repeat it.","whyHi":"Jab ek kaam bahut items ya baar-baar karna ho, same lines copy karna messy aur mistake-prone ho jata hai. Loop repetition ko ek baar likhne deta hai.","analogyEn":"A repeat-until-exit menu is like a shop counter that stays open until the customer says “I am done”. The loop needs a clear stopping signal.","analogyHi":"Repeat-until-exit menu shop counter jaisa hai jo customer ke “done” bolne tak open rehta hai. Loop ko clear stop signal chahiye.","stepsEn":["choice starts as an empty string, so it is not equal to exit.","The loop body runs and updates choice to exit.","The condition is checked again; it is now False, so the loop stops."],"stepsHi":["choice empty string se start hota hai, jo exit ke equal nahi hai.","Loop body run hoti hai aur choice ko exit set karti hai.","Condition dobara check hoti hai; ab False hai, isliye loop stop."],"code":"choice = \"\"\nwhile choice != \"exit\":\n    choice = \"exit\"\nprint(\"Menu closed\")","output":"Menu closed","mistakeEn":"Forgetting to update the control variable is a common cause of infinite loops. Also avoid changing the same list while iterating over it without a clear plan.","mistakeHi":"Control variable update karna bhoolna infinite loop ka common reason hai. List par iterate karte hue usi list ko casually modify mat karo.","memoryEn":"Every input-controlled loop needs: initial value, repeated action, and a way to reach the stop condition.","memoryHi":"Input-controlled loop mein initial value, repeated action aur stop condition tak pahunchne ka tareeka zaroori hai.","conceptEn":"Many loop bugs come from an unchanged condition, an off-by-one range or an update in the wrong place. Input-controlled loops often use a sentinel, a flag or while True with a deliberate break.","conceptHi":"Loop bugs aksar condition update na hone, range ki boundary galat hone ya update galat jagah hone se aate hain. Input-controlled loops mein sentinel, flag ya while True ke saath deliberate break use hota hai."},"strings/01-string-basics-indexing-slicing":{"titleEn":"Let's understand String basics, immutability, indexing and slicing from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo String basics, immutability, indexing and slicing ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"A string is like a row of letter tiles. Each tile has a position, so you can pick one character or take a slice of consecutive characters.","analogyHi":"String letters ki row jaisi hai. Har character ki position hoti hai; ek character ya continuous part access kar sakte ho.","stepsEn":["Index 0 points to the first character P.","Index -1 points to the last character N.","Slice [1:4] starts at index 1 and stops before index 4: Y, T, H."],"stepsHi":["Index 0 first character P ko point karta hai.","Index -1 last character N ko point karta hai.","Slice [1:4] index 1 se start aur 4 se pehle stop hota hai: Y, T, H."],"code":"word = \"PYTHON\"\nprint(word[0])\nprint(word[-1])\nprint(word[1:4])","output":"P\nN\nYTH","mistakeEn":"Strings are immutable. You cannot do word[0] = \"p\"; create a new string with a method or slicing instead.","mistakeHi":"Strings immutable hoti hain. word[0] = \"p\" allowed nahi; method ya slicing se nayi string banao.","memoryEn":"Index picks positions; slicing picks a range; the stop index is excluded.","memoryHi":"Index ek position deta hai; slicing range deti hai; stop index include nahi hota.","conceptEn":"A string is an immutable sequence of Unicode characters. Indexing selects one character; slicing creates a new piece using start:stop:step, with the stop position excluded.","conceptHi":"String Unicode characters ki immutable sequence hoti hai. Indexing ek character nikalti hai; slicing start:stop:step se naya part banati hai aur stop index include nahi hota."},"strings/02-string-methods-case-concatenation-escapes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand String methods, case changes, joining and escape sequences from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo String methods, case changes, joining and escape sequences ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"String methods are like text tools: one changes letter case, one joins pieces, and escape sequences let you put a new line or tab inside text.","analogyHi":"String methods text tools jaise hain: koi case change karta hai, koi text join karta hai, aur escape sequence new line/tab add karta hai.","stepsEn":["upper() returns a new uppercase string.","title() returns a title-cased string.","\\n represents a line break inside the string."],"stepsHi":["upper() nayi uppercase string deta hai.","title() title-case text deta hai.","\\n string ke andar new line represent karta hai."],"code":"name = \"asha\"\nprint(name.upper())\nprint(name.title())\nmessage = \"Hello\\nPython\"\nprint(message)","output":"ASHA\nAsha\nHello\nPython","mistakeEn":"A string method returns a new string; it does not rewrite the original string in place.","mistakeHi":"String method nayi string return karta hai; original string ko in-place change nahi karta.","memoryEn":"Methods act on text; save their returned value if you want to keep the transformed text.","memoryHi":"Methods text par kaam karte hain; transformed text rakhna ho toh returned value save karo.","conceptEn":"Python strings provide methods for changing letter case and useful operators for combining text. Escape sequences represent special characters such as a newline or tab.","conceptHi":"Python strings mein case change karne ke methods aur text jodne ke operators hote hain. Escape sequences newline aur tab jaise special characters ko represent karte hain."},"strings/03-strip-and-replace":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Trimming and replacing text from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Trimming and replacing text ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"Imagine a label with extra spaces around its edges and a typo in the middle. strip() cleans the edges; replace() swaps a chosen piece of text.","analogyHi":"Ek label socho jiske edges par extra spaces hain aur beech mein typo hai. strip() edges clean karta hai; replace() text ka selected part badalta hai.","stepsEn":["strip() removes whitespace from the beginning and end.","replace() creates a new string with the requested text replaced.","The original raw string remains unchanged."],"stepsHi":["strip() beginning aur end se whitespace remove karta hai.","replace() selected text ko replace karke nayi string deta hai.","Original raw string unchanged rehti hai."],"code":"raw = \"  hello world  \"\nclean = raw.strip()\nfixed = clean.replace(\"world\", \"Python\")\nprint(clean)\nprint(fixed)","output":"hello world\nhello Python","mistakeEn":"strip() does not remove spaces in the middle of a sentence. Use replace() or split/join if you need to normalise inner spaces.","mistakeHi":"strip() sentence ke beech ki spaces remove nahi karta. Inner spaces normalize karne ke liye replace() ya split/join dekho.","memoryEn":"strip the edges; replace the exact text you name.","memoryHi":"Edges clean karne ke liye strip; selected text badalne ke liye replace.","conceptEn":"Cleaning text often means removing unwanted characters from its ends or replacing selected text. strip() works at the ends; it does not remove spaces from the middle.","conceptHi":"Text cleaning mein aksar start/end ke unwanted characters hatate hain ya selected text replace karte hain. strip() ends par kaam karta hai; beech ke spaces remove nahi karta."},"strings/04-search-and-validate-strings":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Searching and testing text from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Searching and testing text ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"Searching text is like checking whether a word appears on a page. Validation is like checking whether a form entry follows a simple rule before accepting it.","analogyHi":"Text search page par word dhoondhne jaisa hai. Validation form entry ko accept karne se pehle rule check karna hai.","stepsEn":["in checks whether a substring exists.","endswith() checks the ending of the string.","find() returns the first matching index, or -1 when no match exists."],"stepsHi":["in check karta hai ki substring present hai ya nahi.","endswith() string ka ending check karta hai.","find() first match ka index deta hai; match na mile toh -1."],"code":"email = \"asha@example.com\"\nprint(\"@\" in email)\nprint(email.endswith(\".com\"))\nprint(email.find(\"example\"))","output":"True\nTrue\n5","mistakeEn":"find() can return -1. Do not treat -1 as a valid character position; check the result first.","mistakeHi":"find() -1 bhi return kar sakta hai. -1 ko valid index mat samjho; pehle result check karo.","memoryEn":"Search answers “where or whether?”; validation answers “does this text satisfy my rule?”.","memoryHi":"Search poochta hai “kahan / hai ya nahi?”; validation poochta hai “kya text mera rule follow karta hai?”","conceptEn":"Searching methods locate text or test whether a string has a particular form. Choose find() when a missing match should produce -1, and understand that index() raises ValueError when it cannot find the text.","conceptHi":"Search methods text ko locate karte hain ya check karte hain ki string kisi pattern ko follow karti hai. Match na mile toh find() -1 deta hai; index() match na milne par ValueError raise karta hai."},"strings/05-split-and-join":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Split and join strings from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Split and join strings ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"split() is like cutting a sentence at spaces into word cards. join() is like arranging those cards and connecting them with a separator.","analogyHi":"split() sentence ko spaces par todkar word cards banata hai. join() cards ko separator ke saath wapas jodta hai.","stepsEn":["split() returns a list of words.","The list now has three string items.","The separator string \" | \" joins those items into one string."],"stepsHi":["split() words ki list return karta hai.","Ab list mein teen string items hain.","Separator string \" | \" un items ko ek string mein join karti hai."],"code":"sentence = \"learn Python daily\"\nwords = sentence.split()\nprint(words)\nprint(\" | \".join(words))","output":"['learn', 'Python', 'daily']\nlearn | Python | daily","mistakeEn":"join() is called on the separator string, and the items must be strings. Use map(str, values) when joining numbers.","mistakeHi":"join() separator string par call hota hai aur items strings hone chahiye. Numbers ko join karna ho toh map(str, values) use karo.","memoryEn":"split: one string becomes a list; join: a list of strings becomes one string.","memoryHi":"split: ek string se list; join: strings ki list se ek string.","conceptEn":"split() turns one string into a list of pieces. join() does the reverse: it combines an iterable of strings using the separator on which it is called.","conceptHi":"split() ek string ko pieces ki list mein todta hai. join() iska reverse karta hai: jis separator par call kiya hai, usse strings ko combine karta hai."},"strings/06-format-builtins-loop-processing":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Formatting, built-ins and string processing from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Formatting, built-ins and string processing ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Programs constantly handle names, messages, labels, search text and user input. String skills help you read, clean, search, split, join and present that text correctly.","whyHi":"Programs names, messages, labels, search text aur user input ko baar-baar handle karte hain. String skills se text read, clean, search, split, join aur sahi format mein show kar sakte ho.","analogyEn":"A formatted message is like filling blanks in a ready-made certificate: the sentence stays the same, but the name and score are inserted in the right places.","analogyHi":"Formatted message ready certificate ke blanks fill karne jaisa hai: sentence same rehta hai, name aur score correct jagah insert hote hain.","stepsEn":["The f-string inserts the value of name.",".1f formats score to one digit after the decimal point.","The for loop visits each character in the string one at a time."],"stepsHi":["f-string name ki value sentence mein insert karta hai.",".1f score ko decimal ke baad ek digit tak format karta hai.","for loop string ka har character ek-ek karke visit karta hai."],"code":"name = \"Asha\"\nscore = 92.456\nprint(f\"{name} scored {score:.1f}%\")\n\nword = \"python\"\nfor letter in word:\n    print(letter)","output":"Asha scored 92.5%\np\ny\nt\nh\no\nn","mistakeEn":"Do not forget the f before the opening quote. Without f, {name} is treated as ordinary text.","mistakeHi":"Opening quote se pehle f lagana mat bhoolo. Bina f ke {name} normal text rahega.","memoryEn":"f-string = readable message + values; a string loop = one character per iteration.","memoryHi":"f-string = readable message + values; string loop = har iteration mein ek character.","conceptEn":"f-strings place expressions inside braces for readable formatting. Built-ins and loops help count, classify, reverse or validate text. These patterns are useful for real input cleaning.","conceptHi":"f-strings braces ke andar expressions rakhkar readable formatting dete hain. Built-ins aur loops se text count, classify, reverse ya validate kar sakte hain."},"lists/01-create-index-and-update-lists":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Create, index and update lists from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Create, index and update lists ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"A list is like a numbered shopping list. You can read an item by its position, replace it, and add or remove items while shopping.","analogyHi":"List numbered shopping list jaisi hai. Position se item read kar sakte ho, replace kar sakte ho, aur items add/remove kar sakte ho.","stepsEn":["The list stores three items in order.","Index 0 selects the first item: Maths.","Index 1 is replaced with Science; lists are mutable."],"stepsHi":["List mein teen items order mein store hain.","Index 0 pehla item Maths select karta hai.","Index 1 par Science replace hota hai; list mutable hoti hai."],"code":"subjects = [\"Maths\", \"English\", \"Python\"]\nprint(subjects[0])\nsubjects[1] = \"Science\"\nprint(subjects)","output":"Maths\n['Maths', 'Science', 'Python']","mistakeEn":"Python starts indexing at 0. For three items, valid positive indices are 0, 1 and 2—not 1, 2 and 3.","mistakeHi":"Python indexing 0 se start hoti hai. Teen items ke valid positive index 0, 1, 2 hain; 1, 2, 3 nahi.","memoryEn":"List = ordered + indexed + mutable. Positions start at zero.","memoryHi":"List = ordered + indexed + mutable. Positions zero se start hoti hain.","conceptEn":"A list is an ordered, mutable collection. You can access its items by index and replace an item at a valid position. Lists can hold values of different types.","conceptHi":"List ordered aur mutable collection hoti hai. Index se item access aur valid position par item update kar sakte hain. List mein different data types ke values ho sakte hain."},"lists/02-add-and-remove-list-items":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Add and remove list items from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Add and remove list items ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"Think of a guest list. append adds one guest at the end; extend adds each guest from another group; insert places someone at a chosen position. Removal methods answer different questions.","analogyHi":"Guest list socho. append ek guest end mein add karta hai; extend doosri group ke saare guests add karta hai; insert chosen position par rakhta hai. Removal methods alag situations handle karte hain.","stepsEn":["append() adds one object to the end.","extend() adds each item from the second list.","pop() removes and returns the last item."],"stepsHi":["append() ek object end mein add karta hai.","extend() second list ke har item ko add karta hai.","pop() last item remove karke return karta hai."],"code":"items = [\"pen\", \"book\"]\nitems.append(\"bag\")\nitems.extend([\"bottle\", \"lunchbox\"])\nremoved = items.pop()\nprint(items)\nprint(removed)","output":"['pen', 'book', 'bag', 'bottle']\nlunchbox","mistakeEn":"append([4, 5]) adds the whole list as one nested item; extend([4, 5]) adds 4 and 5 separately.","mistakeHi":"append([4, 5]) poori list ko ek nested item banata hai; extend([4, 5]) 4 aur 5 ko alag add karta hai.","memoryEn":"append one item; extend many items; insert at a position; pop removes and returns.","memoryHi":"append ek item; extend multiple; insert position; pop remove karke value return.","conceptEn":"Lists can grow and shrink. append() adds one object, extend() adds items from an iterable, insert() places an item at a position, and remove/pop/del/clear handle removal in different ways.","conceptHi":"List ka size grow aur shrink kar sakta hai. append() ek object add karta hai, extend() iterable ke items add karta hai, insert() position par add karta hai; remove/pop/del/clear alag tarikon se remove karte hain."},"lists/03-list-functions-membership-and-loops":{"titleEn":"Let's understand List built-ins, membership and loops from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo List built-ins, membership and loops ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"A list is a queue of tasks. len tells you how many tasks there are, in checks whether a task is present, and a loop visits each task.","analogyHi":"List tasks ki queue jaisi hai. len count batata hai, in check karta hai task present hai ya nahi, loop har task visit karta hai.","stepsEn":["len() counts the list items.","in returns True because quiz is present.","The loop assigns each item to task and runs the body."],"stepsHi":["len() list ke items count karta hai.","quiz present hai, isliye in True deta hai.","Loop har item ko task variable mein rakhta hai aur body run karta hai."],"code":"tasks = [\"read\", \"revise\", \"quiz\"]\nprint(len(tasks))\nprint(\"quiz\" in tasks)\nfor task in tasks:\n    print(task)","output":"3\nTrue\nread\nrevise\nquiz","mistakeEn":"Do not call a list method and a built-in function as if they were the same. len(items) is a function; items.count(x) is a method.","mistakeHi":"List method aur built-in function same nahi. len(items) function hai; items.count(x) method hai.","memoryEn":"Functions receive the list: len(items). Methods belong to the list: items.count(x).","memoryHi":"Function ko list pass hoti hai: len(items). Method list ka hota hai: items.count(x).","conceptEn":"Built-in functions answer common questions about lists, while membership tests and loops help inspect every value. enumerate() is useful when both the index and item are needed.","conceptHi":"Built-in functions list ke baare mein common questions ka answer deti hain. Membership aur loops items inspect karte hain. Index aur value dono chahiye hon toh enumerate() useful hai."},"lists/04-list-slicing":{"titleEn":"Let's understand List slicing from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo List slicing ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"Imagine a row of five seats. A slice selects a continuous set of seats by start and stop positions; the stop seat is not included.","analogyHi":"Paanch seats ki row socho. Slice start aur stop positions se continuous seats select karta hai; stop wali seat include nahi hoti.","stepsEn":["[1:4] starts at index 1 and stops before 4.","[:3] uses the default start 0 and stops before 3.","[::2] visits every second item."],"stepsHi":["[1:4] index 1 se start hota hai aur 4 se pehle rukta hai.","[:3] default start 0 leta hai aur 3 se pehle rukta hai.","[::2] har doosra item select karta hai."],"code":"colors = [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\", \"gold\", \"white\"]\nprint(colors[1:4])\nprint(colors[:3])\nprint(colors[::2])","output":"['blue', 'green', 'gold']\n['red', 'blue', 'green']\n['red', 'green', 'white']","mistakeEn":"Remember that the stop index is excluded. This is why items[0:3] gives three items, not four.","mistakeHi":"Stop index exclude hota hai. Isliye items[0:3] teen items deta hai, chaar nahi.","memoryEn":"slice syntax is [start:stop:step]; the stop is a boundary, not a selected index.","memoryHi":"Slice syntax [start:stop:step] hai; stop boundary hai, selected index nahi.","conceptEn":"Slicing selects a range of items and returns a new list. The stop index is excluded, and a step can skip positions or reverse the direction.","conceptHi":"Slicing items ka range select karke nayi list return karti hai. Stop index exclude hota hai; step se items skip ya reverse direction mein read kar sakte hain."},"lists/05-list-aliasing-and-copying":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Aliasing and copying lists from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Aliasing and copying lists ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"Two labels on the same storage box do not create two boxes. If both labels point to one list, changing it through either name changes the same list.","analogyHi":"Ek hi storage box par do labels lagane se do boxes nahi bante. Agar dono names ek list ko point karte hain, kisi bhi name se change karne par same list change hoti hai.","stepsEn":["alias = original makes a second name for the same list object.","Appending through alias changes original too.","copy() creates a separate outer list, so appending to copy does not change original."],"stepsHi":["alias = original same list object ka second name banata hai.","alias se append karne par original bhi change hota hai.","copy() alag outer list banata hai; copy mein append original ko nahi badalta."],"code":"original = [10, 20]\nalias = original\ncopy = original.copy()\nalias.append(30)\ncopy.append(40)\nprint(original)\nprint(copy)","output":"[10, 20, 30]\n[10, 20, 40]","mistakeEn":"A shallow copy separates the outer list but does not recursively copy nested lists inside it.","mistakeHi":"Shallow copy outer list alag banata hai, lekin andar ki nested lists ko recursively copy nahi karta.","memoryEn":"Assignment creates another reference; copy() creates a separate outer list.","memoryHi":"Assignment ek aur reference banata hai; copy() alag outer list banata hai.","conceptEn":"Assignment does not automatically copy a list. Two names can refer to the same object (an alias). Use copy(), slicing or list() for a shallow outer copy, and understand nested mutable objects.","conceptHi":"List assignment automatic copy nahi banata. Do variable names same object ko refer kar sakte hain (alias). copy(), slicing ya list() se shallow outer copy banayein; nested mutable objects ka difference bhi samjhein."},"lists/06-list-sorting-and-reversing":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Sorting and reversing lists from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Sorting and reversing lists ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"Sorting is arranging books alphabetically. Reversing is simply turning the current row around. They are not the same action.","analogyHi":"Sorting books ko alphabetical order mein lagana hai. Reverse ka matlab current order ko ulta kar dena. Dono alag actions hain.","stepsEn":["sorted(scores) returns a new sorted list.","The original scores list is still unchanged after sorted().","sort() changes the original list in place."],"stepsHi":["sorted(scores) nayi sorted list return karta hai.","sorted() ke baad original scores unchanged hai.","sort() original list ko in-place sort karta hai."],"code":"scores = [40, 10, 30, 20]\nprint(sorted(scores))\nprint(scores)\nscores.sort()\nprint(scores)","output":"[10, 20, 30, 40]\n[40, 10, 30, 20]\n[10, 20, 30, 40]","mistakeEn":"sort() changes the list and returns None. Do not write scores = scores.sort().","mistakeHi":"sort() list change karta hai aur None return karta hai. scores = scores.sort() mat likho.","memoryEn":"sorted() gives a new list; list.sort() changes the existing list.","memoryHi":"sorted() nayi list deta hai; list.sort() existing list change karta hai.","conceptEn":"Sorting orders values; reversing only changes their current order. sort() changes the list in place and returns None, while sorted() returns a new sorted list.","conceptHi":"Sorting values ko order mein lagati hai; reversing sirf current order ulta karti hai. sort() original list ko in-place change karke None return karta hai; sorted() nayi sorted list deta hai."},"lists/07-list-comprehensions":{"titleEn":"Let's understand List comprehensions from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo List comprehensions ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"A comprehension is a compact recipe: take each item from a collection, transform it, and optionally keep only items that pass a condition.","analogyHi":"Comprehension compact recipe jaisi hai: har item lo, transform karo, aur chaaho toh sirf condition pass karne wale items rakho.","stepsEn":["for n in numbers visits each number.","n * n creates the transformed value for squares.","The if condition keeps only even numbers in evens."],"stepsHi":["for n in numbers har number visit karta hai.","n * n transformed value create karta hai.","if condition evens list mein sirf even numbers rakhti hai."],"code":"numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nsquares = [n * n for n in numbers]\nevens = [n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]\nprint(squares)\nprint(evens)","output":"[1, 4, 9, 16]\n[2, 4]","mistakeEn":"Do not squeeze a complicated multi-step program into one unreadable comprehension. Use a normal loop when clarity suffers.","mistakeHi":"Complex multi-step logic ko zabardasti ek unreadable comprehension mein mat likho. Clarity kam ho toh normal loop use karo.","memoryEn":"[expression for item in iterable if condition] — read it from left to right.","memoryHi":"[expression for item in iterable if condition] — left se right padho.","conceptEn":"A list comprehension builds a new list from an iterable. It can transform each value and optionally keep only values that satisfy a condition.","conceptHi":"List comprehension iterable se nayi list banati hai. Ye har value transform kar sakti hai aur condition ke basis par selected values hi rakh sakti hai."},"lists/08-nested-lists-and-input-conversion":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Nested lists and input conversion from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Nested lists and input conversion ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"A nested list is like a spreadsheet: the outer list contains rows, and each row contains columns. To reach one cell, choose a row and then a column.","analogyHi":"Nested list spreadsheet jaisi hai: outer list rows rakhti hai, har row columns. Ek cell tak pahunchne ke liye pehle row, phir column choose karo.","stepsEn":["marks[1] selects the second row: [90, 88].","The next [0] selects the first value of that row: 90.","split() creates text pieces; int() converts each piece to a number."],"stepsHi":["marks[1] second row [90, 88] select karta hai.","Uske baad [0] us row ka first value 90 select karta hai.","split() text ke pieces banata hai; int() har piece ko number karta hai."],"code":"marks = [[80, 75], [90, 88]]\nprint(marks[1][0])\ntext = \"12 15 18\"\nnumbers = [int(part) for part in text.split()]\nprint(numbers)","output":"90\n[12, 15, 18]","mistakeEn":"marks[1][0] means row 1, column 0 using zero-based indexing. It does not mean first row, first column.","mistakeHi":"marks[1][0] ka matlab zero-based indexing mein row 1, column 0 hai. Ye first row/first column nahi.","memoryEn":"Nested access = outer index first, inner index second. Convert input pieces before numeric work.","memoryHi":"Nested access = pehle outer index, phir inner index. Numeric work se pehle input pieces convert karo.","conceptEn":"A nested list contains lists as items, often representing rows and columns. split() turns typed text into pieces; convert each piece explicitly when numbers are needed.","conceptHi":"Nested list mein items ke roop mein doosri lists hoti hain, jo rows/columns represent kar sakti hain. split() typed text ko pieces mein todta hai; number chahiye toh har piece convert karein."},"lists/09-list-common-mistakes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Common list mistakes from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Common list mistakes ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"When several related values must stay in order and may change, a list keeps them together. This is useful for marks, shopping items, names, scores and many real program tasks.","whyHi":"Jab related values order mein rakhni hon aur baad mein change bhi karni hon, list unhe ek jagah rakhti hai. Marks, shopping items, names aur scores iske examples hain.","analogyEn":"When checking items in a moving queue, removing a person while walking through the queue can shift everyone’s position. A safer approach is to build a new filtered list.","analogyHi":"Moving queue mein chalte hue beech se item remove karoge toh baaki positions shift ho sakti hain. Safer approach nayi filtered list banana hai.","stepsEn":["The comprehension visits the original list.","Only values with remainder 0 after division by 2 are kept.","A new list is created, so the original is unchanged."],"stepsHi":["Comprehension original list ke har item ko visit karta hai.","2 se divide karne par remainder 0 ho wahi values rakhi jaati hain.","Nayi list banti hai, original unchanged rehti hai."],"code":"numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nevens = [n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]\nprint(evens)\nprint(numbers)","output":"[2, 4, 6]\n[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]","mistakeEn":"Avoid deleting items from the same list while looping over it unless you fully understand the index-shifting effect.","mistakeHi":"Same list par loop chalate waqt usi list se items delete karna avoid karo; indices shift ho sakte hain.","memoryEn":"When filtering, a new list is often clearer and safer than editing the list mid-loop.","memoryHi":"Filtering mein loop ke beech original list edit karne ke bajaye nayi list banana aksar safer hai.","conceptEn":"List bugs often come from confusing in-place methods with returned values, or modifying a list while iterating over it. Learn safer patterns before moving to larger programs.","conceptHi":"List bugs aksar in-place methods ke return value ko galat samajhne ya iterate karte waqt same list remove/change karne se aate hain. Bade programs se pehle safe patterns samjhein."},"tuples_sets/01-tuple-create-and-access":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Tuple basics and accessing items from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Tuple basics and accessing items ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"A tuple is like a printed appointment card: it stores a fixed group of values in order. You can read the fields, but you do not edit an item in place.","analogyHi":"Tuple printed appointment card jaisa hai: fixed values order mein store hoti hain. Read kar sakte ho, lekin item in-place edit nahi kar sakte.","stepsEn":["The tuple stores three values in a fixed order.","Index 0 selects Asha; index -1 selects the last item CSE.","len() counts three items."],"stepsHi":["Tuple teen values fixed order mein store karta hai.","Index 0 se Asha aur -1 se last item CSE milta hai.","len() teen items count karta hai."],"code":"record = (\"Asha\", 18, \"CSE\")\nprint(record[0])\nprint(record[-1])\nprint(len(record))","output":"Asha\nCSE\n3","mistakeEn":"A one-item tuple needs a comma: (5,). Parentheses alone, (5), simply group an integer.","mistakeHi":"One-item tuple ke liye comma chahiye: (5,). Sirf parentheses (5) integer ko group karte hain.","memoryEn":"Tuple = ordered and immutable. A comma creates a one-item tuple.","memoryHi":"Tuple = ordered aur immutable. One-item tuple comma se banta hai.","conceptEn":"A tuple is an ordered sequence. It supports indexing, slicing, membership tests and iteration, but the tuple’s item references cannot be reassigned.","conceptHi":"Tuple ordered sequence hoti hai. Indexing, slicing, membership aur iteration support karti hai, lekin tuple ke item references ko reassign nahi kar sakte."},"tuples_sets/02-tuple-immutability-operations-methods":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Tuple immutability, operations and methods from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Tuple immutability, operations and methods ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"A tuple is a sealed record: you can inspect it and make a new tuple from it, but you cannot replace one of its stored positions.","analogyHi":"Tuple sealed record jaisa hai: inspect kar sakte ho aur usse naya tuple bana sakte ho, par stored position ko replace nahi kar sakte.","stepsEn":["count(4) reports one occurrence.","index(7) reports position 1.","The + operator creates a new tuple; the original stays (4, 7)."],"stepsHi":["count(4) batata hai 4 ek baar hai.","index(7) position 1 batata hai.","+ naya tuple banata hai; original (4, 7) hi rehta hai."],"code":"point = (4, 7)\nprint(point.count(4))\nprint(point.index(7))\nnew_point = point + (9,)\nprint(new_point)","output":"1\n1\n(4, 7, 9)","mistakeEn":"Immutability prevents replacing tuple slots, but a tuple can contain a mutable list whose contents may change.","mistakeHi":"Tuple ke slots replace nahi kar sakte, lekin tuple ke andar mutable list ho toh us list ke contents change ho sakte hain.","memoryEn":"A tuple cannot change its item references, but a mutable object inside it may still change.","memoryHi":"Tuple apne item references change nahi karta, lekin andar ki mutable object change ho sakti hai.","conceptEn":"Tuples are immutable, but they can contain references to mutable objects such as lists. Concatenation, repetition, comparison, count() and index() are useful tuple operations.","conceptHi":"Tuple immutable hoti hai, lekin uske andar mutable object jaise list ka reference ho sakta hai. Concatenation, repetition, comparison, count() aur index() useful operations hain."},"tuples_sets/03-packing-unpacking-and-conversion":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Packing, unpacking and converting collections from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Packing, unpacking and converting collections ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"Packing is putting several values into one parcel. Unpacking is opening the parcel and assigning each item to its own labelled variable.","analogyHi":"Packing kai values ko ek parcel mein rakhna hai. Unpacking parcel kholkar har item ko alag named variable dena hai.","stepsEn":["The tuple stores three values.","Unpacking assigns the first value to name, second to age and third to course.","The number of target names must match the values unless starred unpacking is used."],"stepsHi":["Tuple mein teen values hain.","Unpacking first value name, second age, third course ko assign karta hai.","Normally target names ki count values ke barabar honi chahiye; starred unpacking alag case hai."],"code":"student = (\"Asha\", 18, \"CSE\")\nname, age, course = student\nprint(name)\nprint(age)\nprint(course)","output":"Asha\n18\nCSE","mistakeEn":"If you unpack three values into two names, Python raises ValueError. Use a starred target when one name should collect the remaining items.","mistakeHi":"Teen values ko do names mein unpack karoge toh ValueError aayega. Remaining items collect karne ke liye starred target use hota hai.","memoryEn":"Packing groups values; unpacking distributes them into names.","memoryHi":"Packing values ko group karta hai; unpacking names mein distribute karta hai.","conceptEn":"Packing groups multiple values into a tuple. Unpacking assigns tuple items to matching variables. Lists and tuples can be converted when the program needs a different collection behaviour.","conceptHi":"Packing multiple values ko tuple mein group karta hai. Unpacking tuple items ko matching variables mein assign karta hai. Zarurat ke hisaab se list aur tuple convert kar sakte hain."},"tuples_sets/04-set-basics-and-sequence-differences":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Set basics and how sets differ from sequences from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Set basics and how sets differ from sequences ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"A set is like a guest list where each name appears once. It answers “is this name present?” but it does not promise a numbered position for each name.","analogyHi":"Set guest list jaisa hai jahan har naam ek baar hota hai. Ye “naam present hai?” ka answer deta hai, lekin numbered position promise nahi karta.","stepsEn":["The repeated name Asha is stored only once.","There are three distinct names.","Membership testing checks presence; set indexing is not supported."],"stepsHi":["Repeated Asha sirf ek baar store hota hai.","Total teen unique names hain.","Membership presence check karta hai; set indexing support nahi karta."],"code":"attendees = {\"Asha\", \"Rohan\", \"Asha\", \"Mina\"}\nprint(len(attendees))\nprint(\"Rohan\" in attendees)\nempty_set = set()","output":"3\nTrue","mistakeEn":"{} creates an empty dictionary, not an empty set. Use set() for an empty set.","mistakeHi":"{} empty dictionary banata hai, empty set nahi. Empty set ke liye set() use karo.","memoryEn":"Set = unique items; no positional indexing; use set() for empty.","memoryHi":"Set = unique items; positional indexing nahi; empty ke liye set().","conceptEn":"A set is an unordered collection of distinct hashable items. It removes duplicates and is useful for fast membership-style reasoning, but it does not support positional indexing or slicing.","conceptHi":"Set unordered collection hoti hai jisme distinct hashable items hote hain. Duplicates remove ho jaate hain. Membership ke liye useful hai, lekin indexing/slicing support nahi karti."},"tuples_sets/05-set-add-remove-methods":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Adding and removing set items from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Adding and removing set items ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"A set is a club membership list. add() registers a member, discard() removes only if present without complaining, and remove() reports an error if the member is absent.","analogyHi":"Set club membership list jaisa hai. add() member add karta hai, discard() present ho toh remove karta hai bina complain kiye, remove() absent item par error deta hai.","stepsEn":["add() inserts Mina if it is not already present.","discard() removes Rohan if present and does nothing if it is absent.","sorted() creates a predictable display order for this example."],"stepsHi":["add() Mina add karta hai agar pehle se present nahi.","discard() Rohan present ho toh remove karta hai; absent ho toh error nahi.","sorted() example ko fixed display order deta hai."],"code":"members = {\"Asha\", \"Rohan\"}\nmembers.add(\"Mina\")\nmembers.discard(\"Rohan\")\nprint(sorted(members))","output":"['Asha', 'Mina']","mistakeEn":"set.pop() removes an arbitrary element; do not assume it removes the “last” or a particular item.","mistakeHi":"set.pop() arbitrary item remove karta hai; assume mat karo ki last ya particular item remove hoga.","memoryEn":"add inserts; remove expects presence; discard is safe if absence is possible.","memoryHi":"add insert karta hai; remove ko item present chahiye; absent ho sakta hai toh discard safe hai.","conceptEn":"Sets can be updated using add() for one item and update() for multiple items from an iterable. remove() raises KeyError if absent; discard() does not.","conceptHi":"Set mein ek item add karne ke liye add() aur iterable ke multiple items ke liye update() use hota hai. Value absent ho toh remove() KeyError de sakta hai; discard() nahi."},"tuples_sets/06-set-operations-and-relationships":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Set operations and relationships from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Set operations and relationships ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"Two school clubs may have members in common. Union means everyone in either club; intersection means members in both; difference means members only in the first club.","analogyHi":"Do school clubs ke members common ho sakte hain. Union = dono clubs ke sab unique members; intersection = dono mein common; difference = sirf pehle club ke members.","stepsEn":["| combines all unique items from both sets.","& keeps only shared items.","- keeps items from the left set that are not in the right set."],"stepsHi":["| dono sets ke unique items combine karta hai.","& sirf common items rakhta hai.","- left set ke woh items rakhta hai jo right set mein nahi."],"code":"python = {\"Asha\", \"Rohan\", \"Mina\"}\nsql = {\"Mina\", \"Dev\"}\nprint(sorted(python | sql))\nprint(sorted(python & sql))\nprint(sorted(python - sql))","output":"['Asha', 'Dev', 'Mina', 'Rohan']\n['Mina']\n['Asha', 'Rohan']","mistakeEn":"Difference is directional: A - B may not equal B - A.","mistakeHi":"Difference directional hai: A - B zaroori nahi B - A ke equal ho.","memoryEn":"Union = either; intersection = both; difference = left only; symmetric difference = exactly one side.","memoryHi":"Union = kisi bhi side; intersection = dono; difference = left only; symmetric difference = exactly ek side.","conceptEn":"Set operations model mathematical relationships: union combines both sets, intersection keeps common items, difference keeps left-only items, and symmetric difference keeps items in exactly one set.","conceptHi":"Set operations mathematical relationships ko represent karte hain: union dono sets combine karta hai, intersection common items rakhta hai, difference left-only items, aur symmetric difference exactly ek set mein hone wale items rakhta hai."},"tuples_sets/07-frozenset-comprehensions-and-comparison":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Frozenset, set comprehensions and collection comparison from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Frozenset, set comprehensions and collection comparison ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"Not every collection should behave the same way. Tuples are useful for fixed groups of values; sets are useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter.","whyHi":"Har collection ka behaviour same nahi hona chahiye. Fixed group ke liye tuple useful hai; unique items aur membership check ke liye set useful hai.","analogyEn":"A regular set is a whiteboard list you can edit. A frozenset is a laminated version: it keeps unique items but cannot be changed after creation.","analogyHi":"Normal set whiteboard list jaisa hai jise edit kar sakte ho. Frozenset laminated version jaisa hai: unique items hain par creation ke baad change nahi.","stepsEn":["The set comprehension squares every number.","Repeated input values would not create repeated set items.","frozenset stores unique items but does not offer mutating methods."],"stepsHi":["Set comprehension har number ka square banata hai.","Repeated input values set mein duplicate result nahi banate.","frozenset unique items store karta hai, lekin mutate nahi hota."],"code":"numbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4]\nunique_squares = {n * n for n in numbers}\nlocked = frozenset({\"read\", \"write\"})\nprint(sorted(unique_squares))\nprint(\"read\" in locked)","output":"[1, 4, 9, 16]\nTrue","mistakeEn":"A set comprehension uses curly braces with an expression; a dictionary comprehension uses key: value pairs.","mistakeHi":"Set comprehension mein expression hota hai; dictionary comprehension mein key: value pair hota hai.","memoryEn":"set = unique and mutable; frozenset = unique and immutable.","memoryHi":"set = unique aur mutable; frozenset = unique aur immutable.","conceptEn":"frozenset is an immutable set. Set comprehensions build a set from an iterable. Combining lists and sets helps remove duplicates or find overlap; compare collection properties before choosing one.","conceptHi":"frozenset immutable set hota hai. Set comprehension iterable se set banati hai. Lists aur sets ko combine karke duplicates remove ya common items find kar sakte hain; collection choose karne se pehle properties compare karein."},"modules/01-modules-and-import-styles":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Modules and import styles from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Modules and import styles ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"A module is like a toolbox. Instead of building every tool yourself, you import the toolbox and use the tool you need.","analogyHi":"Module toolbox jaisa hai. Har tool khud banane ke bajaye toolbox import karke required tool use karte ho.","stepsEn":["import math makes the module available under the name math.","math.sqrt accesses the square-root function through that module.","randint is imported directly under the shorter alias roll. Its output varies."],"stepsHi":["import math se module math naam se available hota hai.","math.sqrt module ke through square-root function access karta hai.","randint ko roll alias se import kiya; iska output har run mein badal sakta hai."],"code":"import math\nimport random\nfrom random import randint as roll\nrandom.seed(7)\nprint(math.sqrt(25))\nprint(roll(1, 6))","output":"5.0\n3","mistakeEn":"If you write import math, call math.sqrt(25). Calling sqrt(25) alone will not work unless you imported sqrt directly.","mistakeHi":"Agar import math likha hai toh math.sqrt(25) call karo. Sirf sqrt(25) tab chalega jab sqrt direct import kiya ho.","memoryEn":"Import the module, then use module.name; or import a chosen name directly.","memoryHi":"Module import karo toh module.name; direct name import karo toh name directly.","conceptEn":"A module is a Python file that groups reusable code. import makes names from a module available, and different import styles affect how those names are referenced.","conceptHi":"Module Python file hoti hai jo reusable code group karti hai. import se module ke names available hote hain; import style ke hisaab se unhe refer karne ka tareeka badalta hai."},"modules/02-own-modules-and-main-guard":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Create modules and use the main guard from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Create modules and use the main guard ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"A reusable module is like a shared recipe card. The main guard is the note saying “cook this meal only when this card is run directly, not when another recipe borrows it.”","analogyHi":"Reusable module shared recipe card jaisa hai. Main guard note jaisa hai: is card ko directly run karne par main work karo, import hone par nahi.","stepsEn":["A function definition is created when the module loads.","When run directly, __name__ is \"__main__\" and the guarded block runs.","When imported, the importing module name is used, so the guarded demo does not run."],"stepsHi":["Module load hone par function definition ready hoti hai.","File directly run ho toh __name__ \"__main__\" hota hai aur guard run hota hai.","Import hone par __name__ module name hota hai; demo block automatically run nahi hota."],"code":"# calculator_tools.py\ndef double(value):\n    return value * 2\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(double(5))","output":"10","mistakeEn":"Do not put all reusable functions inside the main guard. Define reusable functions at module level; keep only demo/startup code inside the guard.","mistakeHi":"Saare reusable functions main guard ke andar mat rakho. Functions module level par; demo/startup code guard ke andar rakho.","memoryEn":"Define reusable tools first; protect only the direct-run demo with the main guard.","memoryHi":"Reusable tools pehle define karo; sirf direct-run demo ko main guard se protect karo.","conceptEn":"Reusable code can live in a separate .py file and be imported elsewhere. The __name__ == \"__main__\" guard keeps selected code from running when the module is imported.","conceptHi":"Reusable code ko alag .py file mein rakhkar doosri file mein import kar sakte hain. __name__ == \"__main__\" guard selected code ko module import hone par run hone se rokta hai."},"modules/03-math-roots-powers-rounding":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Math module: roots, powers and rounding from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Math module: roots, powers and rounding ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"The math module is a calculator drawer with specialised buttons for roots, powers and rounding. It saves you from rewriting those formulas yourself.","analogyHi":"math module calculator drawer jaisa hai jisme roots, powers aur rounding ke special buttons hain. Formulas khud likhne ki zarurat kam hoti hai.","stepsEn":["sqrt(49) returns 7.0.","ceil(4.2) returns the smallest integer greater than or equal to 4.2: 5.","floor(4.8) returns the greatest integer less than or equal to 4.8: 4."],"stepsHi":["sqrt(49) 7.0 return karta hai.","ceil(4.2) 4.2 se bada ya equal sabse chhota integer deta hai: 5.","floor(4.8) 4.8 se chhota ya equal sabse bada integer deta hai: 4."],"code":"import math\nprint(math.sqrt(49))\nprint(math.ceil(4.2))\nprint(math.floor(4.8))","output":"7.0\n5\n4","mistakeEn":"ceil and floor are not the same as round. ceil always moves upward to an integer; floor moves downward.","mistakeHi":"ceil aur floor, round ke same nahi. ceil upar integer par; floor neeche integer par le jaata hai.","memoryEn":"ceil = up; floor = down; sqrt = square root. Check the return type too.","memoryHi":"ceil = upar; floor = neeche; sqrt = square root. Return type bhi check karo.","conceptEn":"The math module supplies mathematical functions and constants. Distinguish ceil(), floor(), trunc() and round(): they follow different rules, especially for negative numbers.","conceptHi":"math module mathematical functions aur constants deta hai. ceil(), floor(), trunc() aur round() ke rules alag hain; negative numbers par difference aur clear hota hai."},"modules/04-math-number-theory-logs-trig-distance":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Math module: number theory, logs, trigonometry and distance from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Math module: number theory, logs, trigonometry and distance ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"Different maths questions need different tools: GCD finds the largest shared factor, LCM finds the first shared multiple, and trigonometry measures relationships in a right triangle.","analogyHi":"Alag maths questions ke liye alag tools: GCD largest common factor, LCM first common multiple, trigonometry right triangle relationships calculate karti hai.","stepsEn":["gcd(18, 24) returns 6.","lcm(4, 6) returns 12.","factorial(5) multiplies 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.","pi/2 radians converts to 90 degrees."],"stepsHi":["gcd(18, 24) ka result 6 hai.","lcm(4, 6) ka result 12 hai.","factorial(5) = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.","pi/2 radians ko degrees mein convert karne par 90 aata hai."],"code":"import math\nprint(math.gcd(18, 24))\nprint(math.lcm(4, 6))\nprint(math.factorial(5))\nprint(round(math.degrees(math.pi / 2)))","output":"6\n12\n120\n90","mistakeEn":"math.sin(), math.cos() and math.tan() expect radians. Convert degree input with math.radians().","mistakeHi":"math.sin(), math.cos(), math.tan() radians expect karte hain. Degree input ko math.radians() se convert karo.","memoryEn":"Know which maths question you are answering; for trigonometry, check radians vs degrees.","memoryHi":"Pehle samjho kaunsa maths question solve kar rahe ho; trigonometry mein radians/degrees check karo.","conceptEn":"The math module includes number-theory helpers, logarithms, trigonometric functions and distance calculations. Trigonometric functions use radians, so convert degrees when needed.","conceptHi":"math module number-theory helpers, logarithms, trigonometric functions aur distance calculations deta hai. Trigonometric functions radians use karte hain; zarurat par degrees convert karein."},"modules/05-math-common-mistakes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Common math module mistakes from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Common math module mistakes ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"A calculator only gives the result for the button and units you provide. If you choose the wrong unit or wrong function, the answer may be valid Python but wrong for your problem.","analogyHi":"Calculator wahi result deta hai jo function aur unit tum doge. Galat unit/function choose karoge toh Python valid answer de sakta hai, par problem ke liye wrong.","stepsEn":["The angle begins in degrees.","radians() converts it to the unit expected by math.sin().","isclose() checks approximate equality, which is safer for floating-point results."],"stepsHi":["Angle degrees mein diya hai.","radians() ise math.sin() ke required unit mein convert karta hai.","isclose() approximate equality check karta hai, jo floating-point values ke liye useful hai."],"code":"import math\nangle_degrees = 90\nangle_radians = math.radians(angle_degrees)\nprint(math.isclose(math.sin(angle_radians), 1.0))","output":"True","mistakeEn":"Do not assume decimal calculations always produce exact binary floating-point values. Use isclose() where approximate comparison is appropriate.","mistakeHi":"Decimal calculations hamesha exact binary float nahi dete. Suitable situation mein isclose() use karo.","memoryEn":"Check import style, input type, units and floating-point precision.","memoryHi":"Import style, input type, units aur floating-point precision check karo.","conceptEn":"Common mistakes include forgetting the math. prefix, passing unsupported types, mixing degrees with radians and assuming floating-point results are exact.","conceptHi":"Common mistakes mein math. prefix bhoolna, unsupported type pass karna, degrees-radians mix karna aur floating-point results ko exact maan lena shamil hain."},"modules/06-random-numbers-choices-and-seed":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Random numbers, choices and seed from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Random numbers, choices and seed ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"A random module is like drawing a card from a shuffled deck. You can choose one card, select several without replacement, or shuffle the deck. A seed lets a computer repeat the same pseudo-random sequence for a demo.","analogyHi":"random module shuffled deck se card nikalne jaisa hai. Ek card choose, bina replacement multiple select, ya deck shuffle kar sakte ho. Seed demo ko repeatable pseudo-random sequence deta hai.","stepsEn":["seed(7) sets the starting point for the pseudo-random sequence.","choice() selects one item from the list.","shuffle() rearranges the original list in place; its length stays 3."],"stepsHi":["seed(7) pseudo-random sequence ka starting point set karta hai.","choice() list se ek item select karta hai.","shuffle() original list ko in-place rearrange karta hai; length 3 rehti hai."],"code":"import random\nrandom.seed(7)\nvalues = [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"]\nprint(random.choice(values))\nrandom.shuffle(values)\nprint(len(values))","output":"A repeatable choice for this seed\n3","mistakeEn":"random.shuffle(items) returns None. Call it as a statement, then use the changed list.","mistakeHi":"random.shuffle(items) None return karta hai. Isse statement ki tarah call karo, phir changed list use karo.","memoryEn":"choice = one; sample = several without replacement; shuffle = change order in place.","memoryHi":"choice = ek; sample = bina replacement multiple; shuffle = original order in-place change.","conceptEn":"The random module produces pseudo-random values and selects or rearranges sequence elements. seed() makes a pseudo-random sequence repeatable for a given setup; it does not make randomness secure.","conceptHi":"random module pseudo-random values generate karta hai aur sequence se selection/shuffle kar sakta hai. seed() same setup par sequence repeatable banata hai; security-grade randomness nahi deta."},"modules/07-random-common-mistakes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Common random module mistakes from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Common random module mistakes ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"Random functions have different promises. One gives a number, one chooses an item, and one changes a list. Always ask: what comes back, and did the original object change?","analogyHi":"Random functions alag promises deti hain. Koi number, koi item choose, koi list change. Hamesha poochho: return kya hua aur original object change hua?","stepsEn":["shuffle() changes items in place.","It does not return a new shuffled list; result is None.","The same list still contains three elements."],"stepsHi":["shuffle() items ko in-place change karta hai.","Ye nayi shuffled list return nahi karta; result None hai.","Same list mein ab bhi teen elements hain."],"code":"import random\nitems = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\nresult = random.shuffle(items)\nprint(result is None)\nprint(len(items))","output":"True\n3","mistakeEn":"randint(a, b) includes b, while randrange(a, b) excludes b. Read the boundary rule before using either.","mistakeHi":"randint(a, b) mein b include hota hai; randrange(a, b) mein b exclude. Use se pehle boundary rule dekho.","memoryEn":"Return value and side effect are different things. Check both.","memoryHi":"Return value aur side effect alag cheezein hain. Dono check karo.","conceptEn":"Most random-module mistakes involve range boundaries, replacement rules or misunderstanding in-place mutation. Know what each function returns and whether it changes the input.","conceptHi":"random module ki common mistakes range boundaries, replacement rules aur in-place mutation ko galat samajhne se hoti hain. Har function kya return karta hai aur input change karta hai ya nahi, ye samjhein."},"modules/08-local-global-and-return-values":{"titleEn":"Let's understand Local, global scope and return values from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo Local, global scope and return values ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"A function is like a small work desk. Names created on that desk are local to the function. A global name is kept in the shared room outside the desk. A return value is the finished result handed back.","analogyHi":"Function chhoti work desk jaisa hai. Us desk par bana variable local hai. Global name desk ke bahar shared room mein hai. Return value finished result wapas dena hai.","stepsEn":["price is a parameter and becomes local to the function call.","tax is created locally; tax_rate is read from module scope.","return sends 105.0 back to the caller."],"stepsHi":["price parameter function call ke liye local hota hai.","tax local variable hai; tax_rate module/global scope se read hota hai.","return 105.0 caller ko wapas bhejta hai."],"code":"tax_rate = 0.05\ndef total_with_tax(price):\n    tax = price * tax_rate\n    return price + tax\n\nprint(total_with_tax(100))","output":"105.0","mistakeEn":"print() displays a value; return sends a value back to the code that called the function. They are not interchangeable.","mistakeHi":"print() value screen par dikhata hai; return calling code ko value wapas deta hai. Dono same nahi.","memoryEn":"Local belongs to the function call; return carries a result out.","memoryHi":"Local function call ka hota hai; return result ko bahar bhejta hai.","conceptEn":"Scope describes where a name can be used. Functions can read global names, create local names and return results. Prefer parameters and return values over unnecessary global state.","conceptHi":"Scope batata hai ki variable name program ke kis region mein accessible hai. Function global name read kar sakta hai, local name bana sakta hai aur result return kar sakta hai. Unnecessary global state ke bajaye parameters aur return prefer karein."},"modules/09-nonlocal-and-scope-mistakes":{"titleEn":"Let's understand nonlocal and scope mistakes from the beginning","titleHi":"Chalo nonlocal and scope mistakes ko bilkul basic se samajhte hain","whyEn":"As a program grows, putting every tool in one file becomes difficult to manage. Modules help reuse code; standard-library tools provide tested functionality; scope explains where names can be used.","whyHi":"Program bada hota hai toh saare tools ek file mein rakhna difficult ho jata hai. Modules code reuse karte hain; standard library ready tools deti hai; scope batata hai names kahan use ho sakte hain.","analogyEn":"Imagine a team inside a department. A nested function is a smaller team inside the first team. nonlocal lets the smaller team update a name owned by the enclosing team; global points outside to the module level.","analogyHi":"Department ke andar team, aur us team ke andar chhoti team socho. nonlocal inner function ko enclosing function ka name update karne deta hai; global module-level name ko refer karta hai.","stepsEn":["make_counter creates count and the nested increase function.","nonlocal tells increase to update the count from the enclosing function.","The returned function remembers that count between calls."],"stepsHi":["make_counter count aur nested increase function create karta hai.","nonlocal batata hai ki increase enclosing function ka count update kare.","Returned function calls ke beech count ko remember rakhta hai."],"code":"def make_counter():\n    count = 0\n    def increase():\n        nonlocal count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return increase\n\ncounter = make_counter()\nprint(counter())\nprint(counter())","output":"1\n2","mistakeEn":"global and nonlocal target different scopes. global points to module scope; nonlocal points to the nearest enclosing function scope.","mistakeHi":"global aur nonlocal alag scopes target karte hain. global module scope; nonlocal nearest enclosing function scope.","memoryEn":"global = module level; nonlocal = enclosing function level.","memoryHi":"global = module level; nonlocal = enclosing function level.","conceptEn":"nonlocal applies to nested functions: it rebinds a name in the nearest enclosing function scope. global points to module scope. Confusing these scopes causes common UnboundLocalError and shadowing mistakes.","conceptHi":"nonlocal nested functions mein nearest enclosing function ke name ko rebind karta hai. global module scope refer karta hai. Dono ko confuse karne se UnboundLocalError aur shadowing mistakes aa sakti hain."}};
